class:: ShaderBus
summary:: Visual-rate, language-owned control bus for Shader uniforms
categories:: External>Graphics
related:: Guides/SCShader, Classes/Shader, Classes/ShaderAnalysis

description::
ShaderBus holds one or more floating-point visual controls in code::sclang::. It batches all mapped changes destined for each Shader into one timestamped renderer bundle, so a Pattern, MIDI responder, Routine, or language-side analysis process does not need to issue a separate message for every mapped uniform.

The bus smooths in the language at 60 Hz. A code::smoothing:: value of zero applies a new value on its next scheduled flush; larger values retain more of the previous value at each step. Map any reflected float control, including code::amount::. Bus updates replace an active renderer-side glide for that control. Unlike a server control bus, ShaderBus does not poll code::scsynth::. Use link::Classes/ShaderAnalysis:: when a ShaderBus should receive audio-server analysis.

classmethods::

method:: control

argument:: channels
Number of control channels. Must be a positive Integer. Defaults to 1.

argument:: smoothing
Per-60-Hz-step retained fraction from 0 (immediate) to less than 1 (slower). Defaults to 0.

method:: new

argument:: channels
Number of control channels. Must be a positive Integer. Defaults to 1.

argument:: smoothing
Per-60-Hz-step retained fraction from 0 (immediate) to less than 1 (slower). Defaults to 0.

instancemethods::

method:: set

argument:: value
Finite Float to assign as the channel's destination value.

argument:: channel
Zero-based control channel. Defaults to 0.

discussion::
Schedules a coalesced visual-rate flush. Calling code::set:: several times before the next flush sends only the final destination for that step.

method:: setn

argument:: start
Zero-based first channel. Defaults to 0.

argument:: array
Finite numeric values for consecutive channels.

method:: value

argument:: channel
Zero-based control channel. Defaults to 0.

returns::
The current smoothed value, which can differ briefly from the destination set with code::set::.

method:: map

argument:: shader
Running or pending link::Classes/Shader:: to receive the value.

argument:: uniform
Reflected float uniform name.

argument:: channel
Zero-based control channel. Defaults to 0.

discussion::
Maps one channel to one uniform. Repeating the same Shader/uniform mapping replaces its earlier channel mapping.

method:: unmap

argument:: shader
Previously mapped Shader.

argument:: uniform
Previously mapped uniform name.

method:: flush

argument:: latency
Optional non-negative renderer lead time in seconds. Defaults to each mapped ShaderServer's code::latency::.

discussion::
Advances smoothing once and dispatches a batch immediately. Normal use does not need this method because code::set:: schedules it automatically; it is useful to establish a known value before a performance starts.

method:: free

discussion::
Stops future automatic updates and removes mappings. It does not free mapped Shaders.

examples::

This example uses the packaged extension. Run one demo at a time and wait for setup to finish. It creates its own resources; no earlier example is required. See link::Guides/SCShader:: for the full walkthrough.

code::
(
var source = ShaderDef.filenameSymbol.asString.dirname.dirname +/+ "shaders/fullscreen.wgsl";
v = ShaderServer.new(\busHelp);
v.waitForBoot { |server|
    d = ShaderDef(\helpPulse, source);
    x = Shader(d, [\amount, 0.5], server);
    b = ShaderBus.control(1, 0.15);
    x.map(\amount, b);
    b.set(0.8);
};
)
::

Cleanup before starting another example; wait for any renderer window to close:

code::
(
b.free;
v.clearScheduled;
x.free; d.free; v.free;
)
::

